---
name: graph-dev-task
version: 1.2.0
description: Initial dev pass for a sprint node in graph-orchestration. No prior findings on this node.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - node_id
  - node_order
  - criteria_doc
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - pr_target
  - assignee
  - phase_local
  - ttl_dir
optional_variables:
  - lead
  - cc
  - finding_ids
  - test_command
  - references
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  test_command: "just test"
  references: ""
  finding_ids: ""
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:graph
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" node="{{ node_id }}" order="{{ node_order }}" assignee="{{ assignee }}">
  <description>Implement sprint node {{ node_id }} (order {{ node_order }}) in {{ sprint }}.</description>

  <worktree>{{ worktree_path }}</worktree>
  <branch>{{ branch }}</branch>
  <pr-target>{{ pr_target }}</pr-target>
  <stack-discipline>`{{ branch }}` is a layer cut from the current top of the phase stack; `{{ pr_target }}` is the layer directly below it. Work only on this layer. Never edit, push to, rebase, or open a PR against any layer below, and never wait for a lower layer's QA or CI. If `{{ pr_target }}` gains commits while you work, run `git fetch origin && git merge origin/{{ pr_target }}` once at task start and once before the final push; the parent wins where copies differ. Once this task closes the layer is frozen: every later finding against it is fixed on a new layer cut from the top of the stack, never here.</stack-discipline>
  <acceptance-criteria-doc>{{ criteria_doc }}</acceptance-criteria-doc>

  {% if references %}
  <references>
{{ references }}
  </references>
  {% endif %}

  <workflow>
    <step id="a">Start the task immediately with `atm task start {{ task_id }} "&lt;one line: what you will do first&gt;"`, then begin executing it in the same work session. Make reasonable assumptions and do not ask clarifying questions unless a real blocker prevents execution.</step>
    <step id="b">Before reading code or editing, update from `{{ pr_target }}` first (`git fetch origin && git merge origin/{{ pr_target }}`) so `{{ ttl_dir }}/events.ttl` reflects {{ lead }}'s latest writes, then run `.claude/skills/graph-orchestration/scripts/next-dev-task {{ phase_local }} {{ ttl_dir }}` and retain its JSON. Note: once {{ lead }} appends this task's Assignment event, `{{ node_id }}` is "in-flight" and the cursor query correctly advances past it — do not expect `vars.sprint` to equal `{{ node_id }}`. Instead confirm directly against `{{ ttl_dir }}/events.ttl` that a `triage:Assignment` exists with `triage:ofSprint triage:{{ node_id }}` and `triage:assignedTo` naming you, and that no `triage:Completion` for `triage:{{ node_id }}` exists yet — that is what makes this a valid, currently-assigned task. With an empty `findings` array this is a new/greenfield sprint. Otherwise, `{{ finding_ids }}` must be non-empty and every listed id must appear in `findings` with severity `blocking`; any other result is an invalid assignment—stop and report it. The array is ordered Blocking, Important, Minor.</step>
    <step id="c">Read `{{ criteria_doc }}` before coding. This is the authoritative acceptance criteria for this node. Satisfy every criterion completely — do not weaken or skip any.</step>
    <step id="d">Update the worktree from `{{ pr_target }}` before any edits: `git fetch origin && git merge origin/{{ pr_target }}`.</step>
    <step id="e">Create a private worktree-local task list for this node (not committed; keep it outside the tracked tree). Derive it from `{{ criteria_doc }}`: the deliverables D1..Dn in order, the acceptance tests, and final validation. Do not add items outside the sprint's scope.</step>
    <step id="f">Set a `/goal` to complete all items in the task list. Work through the deliverables, acceptance tests, and final validation in order, updating the list as each item is actually verified (implemented, criterion re-read and satisfied, tests passing). Commit incrementally on `{{ branch }}` as items land. Report a real blocker to {{ lead }} rather than dropping items.</step>
  </workflow>

  <final-actions>
    <step id="0">Critically review the sprint plan and your implementation. Read `{{ criteria_doc }}` again and confirm every acceptance criterion is fully and completely met. Do not proceed if any criterion is unresolved or partially met.</step>
    <step id="1">Run `{{ test_command }}` and confirm it passes with zero failures. Fix any failures before continuing — do not proceed with a failing test suite.</step>
    <step id="2">Commit all remaining changes. Push through the stack, never with plain `git push`: run `git fetch origin && gh stack view --json` (if it reports this branch is not part of a stack, run `gh stack checkout <stack number shown on the PR page>` in this worktree, then repeat), then `gh stack push`. Run `gh stack view --json` again and note the full commit SHA and that `needsRebase` is false for `{{ branch }}`.</step>
    <step id="3">Close the task with the task-close report as the body: `atm task close {{ task_id }} completed --stdin &lt;&lt;'EOF' … EOF` including node `{{ node_id }}`, branch `{{ branch }}`, the full final commit SHA from the push, and confirmation that every task-list item is complete. Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment stays queued and never starts until `{{ task_id }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it. Do NOT append any triage events — {{ lead }} handles all TTL event writes. Use `atm task close {{ task_id }} refused "&lt;reason&gt;"` only when the whole assignment cannot be done; a finding you could not reproduce stays a per-finding note in the `completed` report, never a refusal. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="4">Do not touch any other layer. The layer above `{{ branch }}`, if one exists, merges this branch forward at its own next task start; you never edit a worktree you do not own. This layer is frozen once the task closes; anything found later is fixed on a new layer cut from the top of the stack.</step>
  </final-actions>
</atm-task>
